       On  many  platforms, including x86‐32, socket calls are all multiplexed
       (via glibc wrapper functions) through ssoocckkeettccaallll(2) and similarly  Sys‐
       tem V IPC calls are multiplexed through iippcc(2).

       Although slots are reserved for them in the system call table, the fol‐
       lowing  system  calls  are  not  implemented  in  the  standard kernel:
       aaffss__ssyyssccaallll(2),  bbrreeaakk(2),  ffttiimmee(2),  ggeettppmmssgg(2),  ggttttyy(2),   iiddllee(2),
       lloocckk(2),  mmaaddvviissee11(2), mmppxx(2), pphhyyss(2), pprrooff(2), pprrooffiill(2), ppuuttppmmssgg(2),
       sseeccuurriittyy(2), ssttttyy(2), ttuuxxccaallll(2), uulliimmiitt(2), and vvsseerrvveerr(2)  (see  also
       uunniimmpplleemmeenntteedd(2)).   However,  ffttiimmee(3), pprrooffiill(3), and uulliimmiitt(3) exist
       as library routines.  The slot  for  pphhyyss(2)  is  in  use  since  Linux
       2.1.116   for  uummoouunntt(2);  pphhyyss(2)  will  never  be  implemented.   The
       ggeettppmmssgg(2) and ppuuttppmmssgg(2) calls are  for  kernels  patched  to  support
       STREAMS, and may never be in the standard kernel.

       There  was  briefly sseett__zzoonnee__rreeccllaaiimm(2), added in Linux 2.6.13, and re‐
       moved in Linux 2.6.16; this system call was  never  available  to  user
       space.

